---
/**
 * /about/ — who is behind the figures.
 *
 * /method/ explains how the checking works. This explains who does it, and
 * it is deliberately plain about scale and location. A site that hands people
 * numbers they'll spend money on has to be reachable and honest about what it
 * is. Overstating either would undo the thing the whole site is built on.
 */
import Seo from '../components/Seo.astro';
import Shell from '../components/Shell.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const UPDATED = '2026-07-17';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: `About ${SITE.name}`,
  url: `${SITE.origin}/about/`,
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.origin,
    email: 'cs@farehal.com',
    founder: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Ryan' },
  },
};
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="sitemap" href="/sitemap-index.xml" />
    <Seo
      title="About Flight Policy Desk"
      description="Who checks the figures on this site, from where, and how to reach them. One person, six airlines, no affiliation with any of them and no commission."
      published={new Date('2026-07-17')}
      updated={new Date(UPDATED)}
    />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Shell section={null}>
      <div class="about">
        <h1>About</h1>

        <p class="lede">
          This site is run by one person. My name is Ryan, I'm in USA, and
          I write for people flying in the United States.
        </p>

        <div class="prose">
          <h2>Why it exists</h2>
          <p>
            Airline policy changes constantly and the pages that explain it mostly
            don't. A baggage fee gets quoted in an article, the fee moves, and the
            article stays up — ranking, confident, wrong. Multiply that by six
            carriers and a decade and you get the situation now: search for a bag
            fee and you'll find a dozen answers, several of them stale, none of them
            dated.
          </p>
          <p>
            So this site does one thing that sounds trivial and turns out to be the
            whole point. Every figure carries the date it was read off the airline's
            own page, and a link to that page. If it's old, it says so, in red, on
            the page you're reading.
          </p>

          <h2>Where I'm writing from</h2>
          <p>
            usa. The site is written in English for US travellers, and I want
            that stated rather than implied.
          </p>
          <p>
            Airline policy is a public document. Anyone who reads English, opens the
            carrier's own page, and writes down what it actually says can do this
            accurately — and anyone who skips that step gets it wrong regardless of
            where they sit. What decides whether a figure on this site is right is
            whether I read it from the source and stamped it with the date I read it.
            That's checkable from anywhere, including by you.
          </p>
          <p>
            What I can't offer is first-hand experience of a US airport counter.
            Where a rule has a lived texture the published policy doesn't capture,
            I'd rather you knew who was telling you.
          </p>

          <h2>The scale, plainly</h2>
          <p>
            Six airlines. A handful of guides. One person checking, by hand, opening
            each carrier's page and typing what it says.
          </p>
          <p>
            That's small, and I'd rather say so than dress it up as an editorial team.
            It's also the reason the dates on this site mean something: there is no
            pipeline scraping figures and no queue of drafts going stale. When a page
            says <em>checked 17 July</em>, someone opened the airline's page on 17 July.
          </p>

          <h2>No affiliation, no commission</h2>
          <p>
            I'm not affiliated with Alaska, American, Delta, JetBlue, Southwest,
            United, or any other airline. I don't take commission. Nothing on this
            site is a booking link and no carrier pays to appear here or to appear
            favourably.
          </p>
          <p>
            If that changes, it gets written on this page before it appears anywhere
            else on the site.
          </p>

          <h2>When the airline and I disagree</h2>
          <p>
            They're right and I'm out of date. The link to check is on every data
            card, and using it is the correct response — that's what it's there for.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you find something wrong, tell me:
            <a href="mailto:cs@farehal.com">cs@farehal.com</a>. A correction is
            worth more to this site than a new page.
          </p>

          <h2>More</h2>
          <p>
            <a href="/method/">How we check</a> covers the mechanics: where the
            numbers come from, what the three different dates mean, and what
            happens when a figure goes unchecked too long.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="stamp">Last updated 17 July 2026</p>
      </div>
    </Shell>

    <style>
      .about h1 {
        font-family: var(--font-display);
        font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5vw, 2.5rem);
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: -0.025em;
        color: var(--navy);
        margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
      }
      .lede {
        font-size: 1.12rem;
        line-height: 1.6;
        max-width: 54ch;
        margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
        padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
        border-bottom: 0.5px solid var(--rule);
      }
      .about .prose { max-width: 62ch; }
      .stamp {
        margin-top: 3rem;
        padding-top: 1rem;
        border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule);
        font-family: var(--font-utility);
        font-size: 0.68rem;
        color: var(--slate);
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
